---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import siteData from '../../data/site.json';

const page = siteData.pages.labSignalAtlas;
const styles = [
  'assets/css/tokens.css',
  'assets/css/interior-base.css',
  'assets/css/layout.css',
  'assets/css/components.css',
  'assets/css/services.css',
  'assets/css/spec.css',
];

// The four objects the runtime is built from. Everything the interface renders
// resolves back to one of these, which is what makes the surfaces consistent
// with each other rather than separately maintained.
const objects = [
  {
    name: 'Source',
    holds: 'Reporting, neighborhood writing, and newsletters that cover or reference local places.',
    carries: 'Publication, source family, place reference, time, and a score used for ordering.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Event',
    holds: 'Something happening at a time and a place — live music, civic meetings, listings.',
    carries: 'Start time, venue, place reference, and the publication that reported it.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Place',
    holds: 'The neighborhood spine the whole surface is read against.',
    carries: 'Geometry, a short code, and the pressure count that drives map and ranking.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Mixtape',
    holds: 'A deliberate set binding sources and events into one continuous read.',
    carries: 'Ordered members, a place summary, and a source mix resolved from its members.',
  },
];

// Stated plainly so the page cannot read as a claim of a shipped product.
const state = [
  { name: 'Runtime', value: 'Sources and events are real; Listing records are not yet populated.' },
  { name: 'Mixtapes', value: 'Front-end first. The draft set is local to the browser and is not persisted.' },
  { name: 'Data', value: 'Generated and committed as a static module; the front end makes no runtime fetch.' },
  { name: 'Deployment', value: 'Static-first. No public instance is currently published.' },
];
---
<Base
  title={page.title}
  description={page.description}
  eyebrow={page.eyebrow}
  introKicker={page.introKicker}
  introTitle={page.introTitle}
  introText={page.introText}
  canonical={page.canonical}
  currentNav={page.currentNav}
  wing="lab"
  brandMode="editorial"
  styles={styles}
>
  <section class="section-block section-block--reading">
    <article class="card editorial-card stack">
      <p class="card-kicker">What it is</p>
      <h2 class="section-heading">A city read through one model</h2>
      <p class="section-copy section-copy--large">Signal Atlas collapses local reporting, neighborhood writing, live events, and curated sets into a single reading surface for New Orleans. It answers one question quickly: what is the city paying attention to right now, and where is that attention clustering?</p>
      <p class="section-copy">It is not a dashboard, a chat layer, or a gallery of disconnected views. There is one map, one feed surface, one visual system. The interface is a projection of the underlying model rather than a skin placed over it — which is the whole argument the Lab makes about how these products should be built.</p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="section-block" aria-labelledby="model-title">
    <div class="section-header">
      <p class="card-kicker">World layer</p>
      <h2 class="section-heading" id="model-title">Four objects, one graph</h2>
      <p class="section-copy">Rather than shaping data per view, the runtime defines a small controlled vocabulary and resolves every surface against it. A place is the same place in the map, the grid, and the classifieds rail, because there is only one of it.</p>
    </div>
    <table class="spec-table spec-table--matrix">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Object</th>
          <th scope="col">What it holds</th>
          <th scope="col">What it carries</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {objects.map(row => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{row.name}</th>
            <td>{row.holds}</td>
            <td>{row.carries}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="section-block" aria-labelledby="path-title">
    <div class="section-header">
      <p class="card-kicker">Read path</p>
      <h2 class="section-heading" id="path-title">How information becomes the product</h2>
      <p class="section-copy">Adapters produce a generated runtime module at build time. The renderer never reads that module directly after startup — it queries a graph layer, which is what keeps the map, the grid, and the rail derived from one filtered state instead of three.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="spec-figure">
      <svg class="spec-diagram" viewBox="0 0 720 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="atlas-diagram-title atlas-diagram-desc">
        <title id="atlas-diagram-title">Signal Atlas read path</title>
        <desc id="atlas-diagram-desc">Source adapters produce a generated runtime data module holding the build, feed, and map exports. A graph query layer reads that module and applies filtering, sorting, and place focus. The rendered surfaces — map, main grid, classifieds rail, and mixtape detail — are all projections of that query layer. Nothing is fetched at runtime.</desc>

        <g class="spec-diagram__node">
          <rect x="8" y="120" width="120" height="56" rx="4" />
          <text x="68" y="144">Adapters</text>
          <text x="68" y="162" class="spec-diagram__sub">sources + events</text>
        </g>

        <g class="spec-diagram__node spec-diagram__node--gate">
          <rect x="176" y="86" width="176" height="124" rx="4" />
          <text x="264" y="112">Runtime data</text>
          <text x="264" y="134" class="spec-diagram__sub">signal-atlas-data.js</text>
          <text x="264" y="152" class="spec-diagram__sub">atlasBuild</text>
          <text x="264" y="170" class="spec-diagram__sub">atlasFeed</text>
          <text x="264" y="188" class="spec-diagram__sub">atlasMap</text>
        </g>

        <g class="spec-diagram__node">
          <rect x="400" y="106" width="152" height="84" rx="4" />
          <text x="476" y="132">Query layer</text>
          <text x="476" y="152" class="spec-diagram__sub">createAtlasGraph</text>
          <text x="476" y="170" class="spec-diagram__sub">filter · sort · focus</text>
        </g>

        <g class="spec-diagram__node spec-diagram__node--model">
          <rect x="596" y="112" width="120" height="72" rx="4" />
          <text x="656" y="136">Surfaces</text>
          <text x="656" y="154" class="spec-diagram__sub">map · grid</text>
          <text x="656" y="170" class="spec-diagram__sub">rail · mixtape</text>
        </g>

        <g class="spec-diagram__flow">
          <line x1="128" y1="148" x2="172" y2="148" />
          <line x1="352" y1="148" x2="396" y2="148" />
          <line x1="552" y1="148" x2="592" y2="148" />
        </g>

        <g class="spec-diagram__note">
          <text x="264" y="242">Generated at build time</text>
          <line x1="264" y1="252" x2="264" y2="214" />
        </g>
        <g class="spec-diagram__note">
          <text x="476" y="72">No runtime API dependency</text>
          <line x1="476" y1="80" x2="476" y2="102" />
        </g>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>Everything needed to render the application is present before the first paint. The query layer, not the renderer, owns filtering and ranking.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="section-block" aria-labelledby="surface-title">
    <div class="section-header">
      <p class="card-kicker">Surface</p>
      <h2 class="section-heading" id="surface-title">The read, and what is holding it together</h2>
      <p class="section-copy">The same filtered runtime drives the place-pressure rows, the source and publication counts, and the read-mode controls. When the read changes, every one of those moves with it — they are not separately maintained summaries.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="spec-figure spec-shot">
      <img
        src="/assets/img/signal-atlas/signal-atlas-world-read.png"
        alt="Signal Atlas in world read mode: neighborhood pressure bars for Citywide, Frenchman, CBD, and Riverfront on the left; a field-mix panel counting twelve sources and eight events against seven publications; and a read-mode row switching between World, Local, and Classifieds."
        width="1600"
        height="1100"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>World read mode. Counts under “what is holding this read together” are computed from the visible feed rather than stored.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="section-block" aria-labelledby="state-title">
    <div class="section-header">
      <p class="card-kicker">Present state</p>
      <h2 class="section-heading" id="state-title">Where the build actually stands</h2>
      <p class="section-copy">Signal Atlas is an active build, not a finished product. The honest position is worth more here than the pitch, so the current limits are named rather than left for a reader to discover.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="spec-table__caption">Accurate as of the current runtime export.</p>
    <dl class="doc-defs">
      {state.map(row => (
        <div class="doc-defs__row">
          <dt class="doc-defs__term">{row.name}</dt>
          <dd class="doc-defs__desc">{row.value}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="section-block section-block--reading" aria-labelledby="commission-title">
    <article class="card quiet-panel stack">
      <p class="card-kicker">Why it is the flagship</p>
      <h2 class="section-heading" id="commission-title">The combination is the point</h2>
      <p class="section-copy section-copy--large">Aesthetic direction, information architecture, a structured world model, provenance-aware retrieval, and a rendered product surface were designed as one thing. That combination — not any single layer — is what the Lab is commissioned to do.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button" href="/lab/schedule">Book a free 30-minute consultation</a>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="https://github.com/St-Expedite-Press/on-high-in-blue-tomorrows" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Inspect the source</a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="scripts">
    <script is:inline src="/assets/js/site-shell.js" type="module"></script>
  </Fragment>
</Base>
